    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62127,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:12:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62128,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Patients may present with signs and symptoms related to a pelvic mass, estrogenic manifestations including precocious pseudopuberty or menstrual disturbances {6465418}, or androgenic manifestations. Haemoperitoneum secondary to rupture is an occasional presentation {6465418}. Rare patients have Maffucci&nbsp;syndrome or Ollier disease {1553918; 6365306}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T12:57:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62129,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours usually occur within the first three decades of life (mean patient age: 13&nbsp;years). They account for approximately 5% of all granulosa cell tumours {6465418}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:12:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62130,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:12:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62131,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "<p>Activating alterations in <em>AKT1</em> and <em>GNAS</em> (gsp mutations) have been detected in 60% and 30% of tumours, respectively {26137586; 16507630}. Somatic mosaic <em>IDH1</em> and <em>IDH2</em> mutations have been reported in tumours associated with Ollier disease or Maffucci syndrome {31409083}. Rare reports describe an association with tuberous sclerosis and germline <em>TP53</em> and <em>PTEN</em> mutations {21501861; 16516278; 18669439}. A minority of tumours occur in patients with <em>DICER1</em> syndrome {21501861}. Somatic <em>DICER1</em> mutations are rare {22187960; 26033501; 29275930}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T19:07:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62132,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The vast majority (&gt;&nbsp;95%) of tumours are unilateral, with a mean size of 12&nbsp;cm (range: 3&ndash;32&nbsp;cm). The tumours are typically solid or solid and cystic and rarely predominantly cystic, with a tan to yellow to white cut surface, commonly with areas of haemorrhage (especially if ruptured) {6465418}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:12:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62133,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Tumours display a nodular or diffuse architecture, with scattered interspersed follicles of varying size that have irregular contours and often contain basophilic secretions. Occasionally, a pseudopapillary architecture is noted. Although stroma is typically inconspicuous, there is occasionally extensive sclerosis, particularly in tumours with a nodular growth. Tumour cells have round vesicular nuclei that typically lack grooves and usually have abundant pale or eosinophilic cytoplasm. Tumours with brisk mitotic activity (including atypical forms and nuclear atypia) account for about 10% of cases {6465418}. A variable component of theca cells may be seen. Juvenile granulosa cell tumour may be present as a very minor component of adult granulosa cell or Sertoli cell tumours. Tumours usually express SF1, inhibin, calretinin, WT1, CD99, and CD56, and some express FOXL2 (but lack associated mutation) and EMA.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-23T15:52:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62134,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "<p>See <em>Prognosis and prediction</em>, below.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T14:22:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62135,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:12:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62136,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> primitive granulosa cells with diffuse or nodular growth punctuated by irregularly shaped follicles.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positive staining for sex cord markers.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:12:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62137,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>This entity may be staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:18:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62138,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with tumours confined to the ovary have an excellent prognosis. Rupture, positive cytology, and extraovarian spread are associated with increased risk of recurrence (most often with the first 3&nbsp;years) {6465418}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:12:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]